  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1024 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nA muggy October night is present across Mid-South after another\nrecord-breakingly warm day. Friday will be a different story as a\nstrong cold front, currently extending across southeastern\nMissouri and central Arkansas, sweeps across our area.\nTemperatures and dewpoints will begin to plummet overnight and\ninto the day Friday. Winds will also begin to sustain around 15-\n20 mph with gusts up to 30 mph early Friday and persisting\nthrough sunset. A combination of abnormally dry fuels, decreased\nrelative humidity, and elevated winds will lead to an elevated\nfire danger threat Friday. Avoid any recreational burning and\nproperly extinguish and dispose of cigarettes Friday as wildfires\ncan start easily and spread quickly under the aforementioned\nconditions.\n\nHigh temperatures Friday will be 15-30 degrees lower than\nThursday with readings in the mid 60s to upper 70s. As ample dry\nair and lowered dewpoints will be associated with this cold\nfront, little to no precipitation is expected as this front moves\nacross the Mid-South Friday. Cold air advection and an almost\npolar in nature airmass will trail behind this leading front\nresulting in a below normal weekend with cool mornings and\npleasant days. Sunday morning will be the chilliest over the next\nseven days with areas mainly over West Tennessee seeing readings\nin the upper 30s and the rest of the area in the lower 40s. NBM\nprobs have a 30-50% chance of seeing lows <37 degrees over mainly\nNorthwest Tennessee resulting in potential frost early Sunday\nmorning.\n\nTemperatures will begin to moderate closer to normal by Monday as\ncool high pressure begins to build slightly east of our area.\nEnsembles are hinting at an omega block setting up in the upper\nlevels across the CONUS. Under this pattern, dry weather will\ncontinue over the next seven days and temperatures will begin to\nedge back above normal by Tuesday. This warming pattern will\ncontinue through the end of the week with much of the area seeing\nhighs well into the 80s by as early as Wednesday.\n\nAEH",
